1 NOTE Antiochus IV,  called Epiphanes ("the illustrious") (circa 215-164 bc), 
2 CONT king of Syria (175-164 bc), son of Antiochus III. From 171 to 168 bc, he 
2 CONT was involved in a war against Egypt, defeating two Egyptian kings, Ptolemy 
2 CONT VI and Ptolemy VII. He captured Jerusalem, prohibited Judaism, and tried 
2 CONT to establish the worship of Greek gods. Under the leadership of the Jewish 
2 CONT priest Mattathias (died c. 166 bc) and his sons, the Maccabees, the Jews 
2 CONT revolted (167-160 bc) and drove Antiochus from Jerusalem. Later he won 
2 CONT victories over the Armenians and Persians.
