1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ερμαίος της Ινδίας (Ερμαίος της Βακτρίας) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ο Ερμαίος ήταν γιος του Αμύντα και Έλληνας ηγεμόνας του ινδοελληνικού βασιλείου από το 50 π.Χ. έως το 30 π.Χ. Παντρεύτηκε την Καλλιόπη, κόρη του Ιππόστρατου και ένωσε τα ελληνικά βασίλεια της περιοχής σε ένα, σταματώντας ανταγωνισ
2 CONC μό και διχασμό πολλών χρόνων. Στο τέλος της βασιλείας του το βασίλειο του δεν άντεξε τις νομαδικές επιδρομές και είχε συρρικνωθεί τόσο, ώστε είχε περιοριστεί στην κοιλάδα της Καμπούλ. Τελικά το 30 π.Χ. το βασίλειο κατέρρευσε.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Chinese historical record from the Hanshu Chap. 96A could possible be related to Hermaeus, even though this is very speculative and the record more likely refers to later Saka kings. The chronicle tells how a king who may possib
2 CONC ly be identified as Hermaeus received the support of the Chinese against Indo-Scythian occupants, and may explain why his kingdom was suddenly so prosperous despite the general decline of the Indo-Greeks during the period. The Chi
2 CONC nese records would put Hermaeus's dates later, with his reign ending around 40 BCE.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Hanshu, Chap. 96A, Wutoulao (Spalirises?), king of Jibin (Kophen, upper Kabul Valley), killed some Chinese envoys. After the death of the king, his son (Spaladagames) sent an envoy to China with gifts. The Chinese
2 CONC  general Wen Zhong, commander of the border area in western Gansu, accompanied the escort back. Wutoulao's son plotted to kill Wen Zhong. When Wen Zhong discovered the plot, he allied himself with Yinmofu (Hermaeus?), "son of the 
2 CONC king of Rongqu" (Yonaka, the Greeks). They attacked Jibin (possibly with the support of the Yuezhi, themselves allies of the Chinese since around 100 BCE according to the Hanshu) and killed Wutoulao's son. Yinmofu (Hermaeus?) was 
2 CONC then installed as king of Jibin, as a vassal of the Chinese Empire, and receiving the Chinese seal and ribbon of investiture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Yinmofu (Hermaeus?) himself is recorded to have killed Chinese envoys in the reign of Emperor Yuandi (48–33 BCE), then sent envoys to apologize to the Chinese court, but he was disregarded. During the reign of Emperor Chengd
2 CONC i (51–7 BCE) other envoys were sent, but they were rejected as simple traders.
