1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT =Subhadrangi=
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress consort of the Maurya Empire 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse
2 CONT Bindusara 
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Ashoka
2 CONT Vitashoka
2 CONT
2 CONT Dynasty Maurya 
2 CONT
2 CONT Religion Ajivika 
2 CONT
2 CONT Subhadrangi (also known as Dharma or Janapadakalyani) was, according to Buddhist sources, a wife of the Mauryan emperor, Bindusara and the mother of his successor, Ashoka. The Ashokavadana states that Subhadrangi was the daughter 
2 CONC of a Brahmin from the city of Champa. The legends state that palace politics kept her away from Bindusara and when she finally gained access to him and bore him a son, she is said to have exclaimed, "I am now without sorrow", whic
2 CONC h led to the child being named Ashoka. The name of her second son, Vitashoka (meaning sorrow terminated) has a similar provenance.[1]:332[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Divyavadana calls her Dharma while the Vamsatthapakasini, a 10th-century commentary on the Mahavamsa,[1]:321 names her as Janapadakalyani.[1]:332
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular culture[edit] Subhadrangi's role is played by Pallavi Subhash in the TV series, Chakravartin Ashoka Samrat[3] She was portrayed by Subhashini Ali in the 2001 film Aśoka.
2 CONT
2 CONT References[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Jump up to: a b c Singh, Upinder (2008). A history of ancient and early medieval India : from the Stone Age to the 12th century. New Delhi: Pearson Education. pp. 321–332. ISBN 9788131711200. Retrieved 8 September 2015. 2.Jump
2 CONC  up ^ Thapar, Romila (1961). Aśoka and the decline of the Mauryas (2nd ed.). New Delhi: Oxford University Press. p. 21. Retrieved 8 September 2015. 3.Jump up ^ Playing onscreen mother was a challenge: Pallavi Subhash, IBN Live, 31
2 CONC  January 2015
2 CONT
