1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Vitashoka=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Vitashoka or Tissa (born 3rd-century BCE) was a prince of the Maurya Empire as the only uterine of Ashoka, and the only brother left alive by Ashoka. According to Divyavadana, he was a follower of the Tirthikas and used to critici
2 CONC ze the Buddhist monks for living a comfortable life. He was made to sit on the throne by the courtiers. When Ashoka found out about that, he persuaded Vitashoka to become a Buddhist. Vitashoka became a monk and practised austeriti
2 CONC es rigorously.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Name'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Vitashoka is referred to as Tissa (or Tisya) in Sri Lankan chronicles. Theragatha commentary regards Tissa and Vitashoka as different individuals. Other sources call him Vigatāshoka, Sudatta, or Sugatra. The Mahavamsa later names 
2 CONC him as Ekavihārika.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In the Divyavadana'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Divyavadana narrates a story of someone in Pundravardhana and then again at Pataliputra who drew a picture of the Buddha bowing before Nirgrantha Nataputta. As a punishment, Ashoka ordered the Ajivikas to be put to death and decla
2 CONC red a reward for killing of Nirgranthas. Someone killed Vitashoka taking him to be a Nirgrantha. His head was taken to Ashoka. After identifying that it was his own brother, Ashoka stopped giving orders for executions.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitashoka
