1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Cassander,''' King of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonia_%28ancient_kingdom%29 Macedonia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassander (Greek: Κάσσανδρος Ἀντίπατρος, Kassandros Antipatros; ca. 350 BC – 297 BC), King of Macedonia (305–297 BC), was a son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Antipater/6000000008408116281 Antipater], and founder of the [http://en
2 CONC .wikipedia.org/wiki/Antipatrid_dynasty Antipatrid dynasty]. He was the namesake of his paternal uncle, Cassander.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Early history===
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth, Cassander was taught by the philosopher [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aristotle Aristotle] at the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyceum_%28Classical%29 Lyceum] in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greece Greece].
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassander is first recorded as arriving at [http://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-III-Argaiados/6000000007875679261 Alexander the Great’s] court in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babylon Babylon] in 323 BC, where he had been sent by
2 CONC  his father, Antipater, most likely to the help uphold Antipater’s regency in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonia_%28ancient_kingdom%29 Macedon], although a later contemporary who was hostile to the Antipatrids suggested that 
2 CONC Cassander had journeyed to the court to poison the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whatever the truth of this suggestion, Cassander stood out amongst the diadochi in his hostility to Alexander's memory. As Cassander and the other diadochi struggled for power, [http://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-IV-Aegus/600000
2 CONC 0003645896957 Alexander IV], [http://www.geni.com/people/Roxana/6000000000351102159 Roxana], and Alexander’s supposed illegitimate son [http://www.geni.com/people/Heracles-of-Macedonia/6000000003645896941 Heracles] were all execut
2 CONC ed on Cassander's orders, and a guarantee to Olympias to spare her life was not respected. Cassander's decision to restore Thebes, which had been destroyed under Alexander, was perceived at the time to be a snub to the deceased Ki
2 CONC ng. It was even said that he could not pass a statue of Alexander without feeling faint. Cassander has been perceived to be ambitious and unscrupulous, and even members of his own family were estranged from him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Later history===
2 CONT
2 CONT As Antipater grew close to death in 319 BC, he transferred the regency of Macedon not to Cassander, but to Polyperchon, possibly so as not to alarm the other diadochi through an apparent move towards dynastic ambition, but perhaps
2 CONC  also because of Cassander’s own ambitions. Cassander rejected his father’s decision, and immediately went to seek the support of Antigonus, Ptolemy and Lysimachus as his allies. Waging war on Polyperchon, Cassander destroyed his 
2 CONC fleet, put Athens under the control of [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrius_of_Phaleron Demetrius of Phaleron], and declared himself Regent in 317 BC. After Olympias’ successful move against [http://www.geni.com/people/Philip-I
2 CONC II-Arrhidaeus/6000000002188198744 Philip III] later in the year, Cassander besieged her in Pydna. When the city fell two years later, [http://www.geni.com/people/Olympias-Aeacid/6000000000351102192 Olympias] was killed, and Cassan
2 CONC der had Alexander IV and Roxanne confined at Amphipolis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassander associated himself with the Argead dynasty by marrying Alexander’s half-sister, Thessalonica, and he had Alexander IV and Roxanne executed in either 310 BC or the following year. By 309 BC, Polyperchon began to claim tha
2 CONC t Heracles was the true heir to the Macedonian inheritance, at which point Cassander bribed him to have the boy killed. After this, Cassander’s position in Greece and Macedonia was reasonably secure, and proclaimed himself King in
2 CONC  305 BC. After the Battle of Ipsus in 301 BC, in which Antigonus was killed, he was undisputed in his control of Macedonia; however, he had little time to savour the fact, dying of dropsy in 297 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassander’s dynasty did not live much beyond his death, with his son Philip dying of natural causes, and his other sons Alexander and Antipater becoming involved in a destructive dynastic struggle along with their mother. When Ale
2 CONC xander was ousted as joint king by his brother, [http://www.geni.com/people/Demetrius-I-Poliorcetes/6000000007265083885 Demetrius I] took up Alexander's appeal for aid and ousted Antipater II, killed Alexander V and established th
2 CONC e Antigonid dynasty. The remaining Antipatrids, such as Antipater Etesias, were unable to re-establish the Antipatrids on the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of more lasting significance was Cassander’s refoundation of Therma into Thessalonica, naming the city after his wife. Cassander also founded Cassandreia upon the ruins of Potidaea.
2 CONT
