1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --?-- Of Commagene's father was Antiochus of Commagene I and her mother was Isias Philostrogos of Cappadocia.  Her paternal grandparents were Mithridates of Commagene I and Laodike Thea Philadelphos. She had a brot
2 CONC her named Mithridates.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laodice_of_Parthia
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Laodice of Parthia=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Laodice (Greek: η Λαοδίκη) was a Princess from the Kingdom of Commagene who lived in the 1st century BC. She was of Greek and Armenian descent.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Laodice was the first daughter born to King Antiochus I Theos of Commagene and Queen Isias Philostorgos.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married King Orodes II of Parthia. Through this marriage, she became Queen of the Parthian Empire. In 38 BC Laodice and Orodes with their children, were in a parricidal and fratricidal massacre perpetrated by Phraates IV of Pa
2 CONC rthia to whom Orodes II handed over the Parthian throne after the death of Laodice and Orodes’ son Pacorus I of Parthia in battle in 38 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Laodice died, her brother King Mithridates II of Commagene had her body returned to Commagene. She was buried near her mother Isias, her sister Antiochis of Commagene and her niece Aka I of Commagene. Mithridates in her honou
2 CONC r built and dedicated a funeral monument.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tomb tumulus of Laodice measures 21 metres or 69 feet. Only one column is still standing with a stele on top of it. The stele depicts a dexiosis relief or a scene between Mithridates II and Laodice shaking hands. The inscripti
2 CONC on underneath the dexiosis relief is so weathered that the inscription wasn’t notice until 1938. It wasn’t until 1979; the inscription was finally recorded and revealed:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The great King Mithridates, the son of the great King Antiochus and Queen Isias, dedicated this image to the unfading memory of Queen Laodice, the king’s sister and the wife of Orodes, the king of kings, and to her own honour.''
2 CONT
2 CONT This inscription dedicated to Laodice suggests a cenotaph, as Mithridates II is saying farewell to his sister, Laodice. The grave chamber of Laodice was located inside the tumulus. After the Kingdom of Commagene was annexed in 72 
2 CONC by the Roman Emperor Vespasian, her tomb was plundered. Romans removed stone blocks from her tomb and used the stones for construction projects in Commagene.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laodice_of_Parthia
