2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE Cambyses II (reigned 529-522 bc), king of Persia, son of Cyrus the Great, 
2 CONT whom he succeeded. In order to maintain control over the Persian Empire, 
2 CONT Cambyses had his younger brother, Smerdis (died about 523 bc), murdered. 
2 CONT He then led an expedition to conquer Egypt, the sole independent kingdom 
2 CONT in the Middle East after the conquest of Asia by his father. Cambyses 
2 CONT defeated Psamtik III, king of Egypt (reigned 526-525 bc), and succeeded in 
2 CONT conquering Egypt as far south as Nubia, but he failed in later attacks on 
2 CONT the Egyptian oasis of Ammonium (now Siwa) and in campaigns in Ethiopia. 
2 CONT During his absence in Egypt, a usurper, Gaumata (reigned 522 bc), claimed 
2 CONT to be Smerdis and seized the throne of Persia. The death of Smerdis had 
2 CONT been kept secret; so Gaumata's claim was believed, and he was acknowledged 
2 CONT king throughout Persia for about seven months. Cambyses was on his way to 
2 CONT Persia to punish Gaumata when he either died by accident or committed 
2 CONT suicide. According to Herodotus, Cambyses II was a dissolute and inhuman 
2 CONT despot, prone to drunken or insane rages in which he committed 
2 CONT sacrilegious and cruel acts.
