1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King of Macedonia 359-336 BCE; Greek General; four wives; father of Alex the
2 CONT Great, Philip III Arrhidaios, Cynane, Thessalonike, and Cleopatra of Macedonia
1 NOTE BORN 383/2 BC; KING OF THE MACEDONIANS 359 BC-336 BC; MARRIAGE DATES AS
2 CONT FOLLOWS: PHILA(-), AUDATA-EURYDIKE (358 BC), PHILINNA (358 BC), OLYMPIAS (357
2 CONT BC), NIKESIPOLIS (353 BC), MEDA (339 BC), KLEOPATRA (338 BC); ASSASSINATED
2 CONT 6/336 BC
1 NOTE From 367 to 365, Philip was a hostage in Thebes, and during that period he 
2 CONT observed the military techniques of Thebes, then the greatest power in 
2 CONT Greece. In 364 he returned to Macedonia. In 359 he was made regent for his 
2 CONT infant nephew Amyntas; later that year he seized the throne for himself. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Faced by internal dissensions and attacked on all sides, he reorganized 
2 CONT the Macedonian army on the model of the Theban phalanx. In less than two 
2 CONT years he had secured the safety of his kingdom and firmly established 
2 CONT himself on the throne. From then on his policy was aggressive. In 357 he 
2 CONT conquered the Athenian colony of Amphipolis in Thrace, gaining possession 
2 CONT of the gold mines of Mount Pangaeus, which financed his subsequent wars. 
2 CONT In 356 he captured Potidea in Chalcidice and Pydna on the Thermaic Gulf. 
2 CONT In 355 he captured the Thracian town of Crenides, which, under its new 
2 CONT name, Philippi, soon acquired great wealth and fame. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 354 Philip conquered Methone and then advanced into Thessaly. By 352 he 
2 CONT had reached the pass of Thermopylae, which, however, he did not attempt to 
2 CONT take, because it was strongly guarded by the Athenians. In 351 the great 
2 CONT Athenian orator Demosthenes delivered the first of his Philippics, a 
2 CONT series of speeches warning the Greeks about the Macedonian menace to Greek 
2 CONT liberty. By 348 Philip had conquered Thrace and Chalcidice. Two years 
2 CONT later he made peace with Athens, which had been at war with him in defense 
2 CONT of its ally, the Chalcidian city Olynthus. Philip was next requested by 
2 CONT the Thebans to interfere in the sacred war against Phocis. He marched into 
2 CONT Phocis in 346 and destroyed its cities. Thereafter Macedonia replaced 
2 CONT Phocis in the Amphictyonic League, giving Philip the right to participate 
2 CONT in Greek political affairs; in 338 the council appointed Philip commander 
2 CONT of the league forces. The Athenians, aroused by Demosthenes, united with 
2 CONT the Thebans against Philip, but their combined army was utterly defeated 
2 CONT in 338 at the Battle of Chaeronea. Philip's victory made him complete 
2 CONT master of Greece and ended Greek independence. Two years later, while 
2 CONT preparing to invade Persia, he was assassinated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip was the greatest statesman and general of his time. He laid the 
2 CONT foundation of the Macedonian military power employed by his son, Alexander 
2 CONT the Great, to conquer and Hellenize the Middle East. A treasure-filled 
2 CONT royal tomb, believed to be Philip's, was excavated at Vergina, near 
2 CONT Thessaloníka, Greece, in 1977.
1 NOTE He rulded Macedonia from 394 to 370 b.c. His descendants include Roman Emperor Maximianus.
1 NOTE He rulded Macedonia from 394 to 370 b.c. His descendants include Roman Emperor Maximianus.
