2 CONT '''Innin-etirat''' (Akkadian: Innin-ēṭirat)[98] – attested as "the king's daughter" in a 564 BC document which records her granting mār-banûtu status[98] ("status of a free man")[99] to a slave by the name Nabû-mukkê-elip.[98] The
2 CONC  document in question was written at Babylon, but names including the divine prefix Innin are almost unique to Uruk, suggesting that she was a resident of that city.[30]
