1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ashur-etil-ilaniFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Ashur-etil-ilani King of Assyria Reign ca. 631 – ca. 627 BC Died 627 BC Predecessor Ashurbanipal Successor Sin-shumu-lishir Father As
2 CONC hurbanipal 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ashur-etil-ilani was a king of Assyria (ca. 631 BC - ca. 627 BC). He succeeded his father Ashurbanipal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide] 1 Problems with source material 2 Reign 3 Dating his reign 4 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Problems with source materialThe reconstruction of the events occurring during Ashur-etil-ilani's rule has proven to be very difficult. One noteworthy problem is the complete absence of sources from central Assyria covering
2 CONC  this time, and the lack of official recordings of events (e.g. royal inscriptions). The most important source for this period is the "Nabopolassar Chronicle", which, however, is quite fragmentary for this period.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] ReignThe death of Ashurbanipal between 631 and 627 BC opened the way for a serious struggle for the control of Assyria between several pretenders which was to lead to the downfall of the Neo Assyrian Empire. The contest may
2 CONC  have actually begun a few years earlier during Ashurbanipal's lifetime. The development of the events, and even the number of parties involved is not known with certainty. It does seem certain that upon Ashurbanipal's death, alli
2 CONC ed hordes of Scythians, Cimmerians, Medes and Persians, taking advantage of Assyria's weakness due to internal strife, crossed the borders of the Assyrian Empire destroying Ashkelon and raiding as far as Egypt. Calah (Nimrud) was 
2 CONC burned, but the strong walls of Nineveh protected the remnants of the Assyrian army that had taken refuge behind them. When the raiders had passed on to other regions, a new palace was erected among the ruins of the neighbouring c
2 CONC ity. But its architectural poverty and small size show that the resources of Assyria were at a low ebb.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Dating his reignAccording to the Harran Inscription of Nabonidus, Ashur-etil-ilani reigned for three years, but there is a contract from Nippur dated to his fourth year. It thus seems that he succeeded Ashurbanipal in 627 B
2 CONC C and ruled until 623 BC. This raises some problems over the dating of events from the Assyrian-Babylonian war.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ashur-etil-ilani must therefore have succeeded to the Assyrian throne before 627 BC. It has been suggested that his reign actually overlapped with Ashurbanipal's. However, it is more likely that Ashurbanipal died before 627 BC bec
2 CONC ause there is no evidence of a co-regency. Therefore, it has been suggested that Ashurbanipal died in 631 BC and that Ashur-etil-ilani was the Assyrian king until 627 BC.[2] There are still issues over some dates which conflict wi
2 CONC th this conclusion, but these dates seem to best support the available evidence.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by Sin-shumu-lishir who deposed him.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes1.^ See for this discussion S. Zawadzki, The Fall of Assyria p. 39-41 2.^ N. Na'aman, ZA 81 1991 3.^ S. Zawadzki, ZA 85 1995 p. 71-73 Preceded by Ashurbanipal King of Assyria ca. 631–627 BC Succeeded by Sin-shumu-lishi
