1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Anicius Auchenius Bassus''' (floruit 382-384) was a politician and judge of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography: Bassus was a native of Beneventum and patron of that city,[1] as well as of Fabrateria Vetus[2] and, by family tradition, of Naples.[3] He belonged to the gentes Anicia and Auchenia; in some inscriptions[4] he is called
2 CONC  "restitutor generis Aniciorum", a possible reference to the fact that for some time he was the only male in the family, before he married and had sons, adoption, or restoration of position or reputation of the family. [5] It is k
2 CONC nown that both his father and his grandfather were consuls;[6] for this reason, his father has been identified with Amnius Manius Caesonius Nicomachus Anicius Paulinus, consul in 334, his grandfather with Amnius Anicius Iulianus, 
2 CONC consul in 322.[5] He married Turrenia Honorata and had several sons;[7] two of them have been identified with the Anicius Auchenius Bassus attested as consul in 408 and with Tyrrenia Anicia Iuliana.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT His career is known thanks to an inscription.[6] At the beginning of the career he was questor candidatus and pretor tutelaris, a personal unification of several offices. Between 372 and 382 is to be dated his next office, the one
2 CONC  of proconsul Campaniae, governor of Campania; in this capacity he is attested to have repaired the bath of Antium (CIL X, 6656). Between November 22, 382,[8] and August 25, 383[9] he was praefectus urbi of Rome. In this capacity 
2 CONC he investigated a dispute between Cyriades and Auxentius about the building of a bridge[10] and a debt to the arcs vinaria (the money for wine donations to the people).[11] In 384 he was investigated for possible misconduct in his
2 CONC  office.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bassus was a Christian;[7] he probably was the judge in a trial for heresy against Bishop Ephesius, of the Luciferian current, whom he acquitted.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Anicius Auchenius Bassus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Anicius Auchenius Bassus (fl. 408) was a politician of the Roman Empire. In 408 he was appointed consul. According to B.L. Twyman, he represents the "mainline" of the gens Anicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bassus was probably the son of the Anicius Auchenius Bassus who was praefectus urbi in 382-383 and of Turrenia Honorata. He had a son, also called Anicius Auchenius Bassus, consul in 431. He wrote the epigraph for the tomb of Moni
2 CONC ca, Augustine of Hippo's mother. The actual stone on which it was written was rediscovered in 1945 in the church of Santa Aurea, in Ostia Antica.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anicius_Auchenius_Bassus_(consul_408)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Anicius Auchenius Bassus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Anicius Auchenius Bassus (floruit 382-384) was a politician of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Bassus was a native of Beneventum and patron of that city, as well as of Fabrateria Vetus and, by family tradition, of Naples. He belonged to the gentes Anicia and Auchenia; in some inscriptions he is called "restitutor generis An
2 CONC iciorum", a reference either to the fact that he was adopted into the Anicii when no other male members were still alive or, more probably, that for some time he was the only male in the family, before he married and had sons. It 
2 CONC is known that both his father and his grandfather were consuls; for this reason, his father has been identified with Amnius Manius Caesonius Nicomachus Anicius Paulinus, consul in 334, his grandfather with Amnius Anicius Iulianus,
2 CONC  consul in 322. He married Turrenia Honorata and had several sons; two of them have been identified with the Anicius Auchenius Bassus attested as consul in 408 and with Tyrrenia Anicia Iuliana.
2 CONT
2 CONT His career is known thanks to an inscription.[6] At the beginning of the career he was questor candidatus and pretor tutelaris, a personal unification of several offices. Between 372 and 382 is to be dated his next office, the one
2 CONC  of proconsul Campaniae, governor of Campania; in this capacity he is attested to have repaired the bath of Antium (CIL X, 6656). Between November 22, 382, and August 25, 383 he was praefectus urbi of Rome. In this capacity he inv
2 CONC estigated a dispute between Cyriades and Auxentius about the building of a bridge and a debt to the arcs vinaria (the money for wine donations to the people). In 384 he was investigated for possible misconduct in his office.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bassus was a Christian; he probably was the judge in a trial for heresy against bishop Ephesius, of the Luciferian current, whom he acquitted.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anicius_Auchenius_Bassus_(prefect)
