1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Demetrias=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetrias (fl. 413-440) was a Roman noblewoman, member of the powerful family of the Anicii and acquaintance of several churchmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Anicia Demetrias, born around 398, was the daughter of Anicius Hermogenianus Olybrius, consul in 395, and of Anicia Iuliana, and thus member of the noble gentes Anicia and Amnia; she is described as the noblest and richest person 
2 CONC in the Roman world in the 410s.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 410, in occasion of the sack of Rome, Demetrias left Rome with her mother Iuliana and with her paternal grandmother Anicia Faltonia Proba and went to Carthage; here they were imprisoned by the comes Heraclianus, and freed only 
2 CONC after a huge payment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother and her grandmother, while they lived in Carthage, come in contact with Augustine of Hippo, who helped them to follow the path of a religious life. Demetrias, who was about fifteen years old in 413, was to be married, b
2 CONC ut she secretly followed an ascetical way of life, influenced by Augustine. She did not tell about her choice to her relatives, as she feared to displease them, but as her marriage was near, she decided to tell her mother Iuliana 
2 CONC and her grandmother Proba about her intention to renounce to marry and to take the veil. Her relatives were very happy with her, and, in 413, Demetrias took the veil in a ceremony celebrated by Bishop Aurelius of Carthage.
2 CONT
2 CONT To help her in her spiritual life, Iuliana and Proba asked several churchmen to send Demetrias advices. Augustine answered suggesting the reading of his De sancta virginitate, Jerome sent a long letter with several advices, while 
2 CONC Pelagius, a theologian opposed by Augustine, addressed her a treaty under the form of a letter, the Epistola ad Demetriam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later in her life, Demetrias returned to her native city of Rome. In this period she received the Epistula ad Demetriadem de vera humilitate, written in 440 by Pope Leo I or, according to recent studies, in 435 by Prosper of Aquit
2 CONC aine, and attacking Pelagius' doctrine on the basis of Augustine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Demetrias built a church devoted to Saint Stephen along the Via Latina (41°51′41.81″N 12°32′15.17″E ), at three miles from Rome, on one of her properties. She died during the pontificate of Pope Leo I.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demetrias_(daughter_of_Anicius_Hermogenianus_Olybrius)
