2 CONT Bagrat II (Georgian: ბაგრატ II) (937–994) was a Georgian prince of the Bagratid dynasty of Tao-Klarjeti and the titular king of Iberia-Kartli from 958 until his death. He was also known as Bagrat Regueni (ბაგრატ რეგუენი), "Regueni
2 CONC " being a moniker rendered in English as "the Simple".
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrat was the elder son of Sumbat I, whom he succeeded as “king of the Iberians” in 958. In spite of having a royal status, Bagrat only ruled northern or Hither Tao and, unlike his father, was not bestowed with the high Byzantine
2 CONC  court title of curopalates, which was granted to Bagrat’s cousin and the ruler of southern or Thither Tao, David III, the most influential person among the Bagratids of that time. Professor Taqaishvili explains Bagrat’s epithet “
2 CONC Regueni” by his younger age at his ascension to the throne. Bagrat frequently appeared as a collaborator of David, aiding him against the Rawadids of Azerbaijan and even against his own son and co-king Gurgen during a brief split 
2 CONC among the Bagratids. By 978, Gurgen had become a de facto king of Iberia, while his son Bagrat III had been adopted and designed as heir by David III of Tao, thus setting stage for future unification of various Georgian polities i
2 CONC nto a single Bagratid realm.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bagrat had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gurgen (died 1008), his successor as king of Iberia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sumbat (died 992)
2 CONT
