1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Adarnase II, sometimes known as Adarnase I,[1] (Georgian: ადარნასე) was a Georgian Bagratid prince and a co-ruler of Tao-Klarjeti with his brothers — Bagrat I Kuropalates and Guaram Mampali — with the title of eris
2 CONC tavt-eristavi ("duke of dukes") (830-c.870).
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase was the oldest son of the Georgian presiding prince Ashot I and inherited all the lands west to the Arsiani Range with the exception of Shavsheti and Lower Tao (now in Turkey).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase was married to "Bevreli", a daughter of the Abasgian king Bagrat I. At some point, she was forced by Adarnase into retirement to a monastery where she lived and died as Anastasia. After Adarnase's death c. 870, his posses
2 CONC sions were equally divided among his sons: Gurgen obtained Tao, while Sumbat received Klarjeti. Adarnase's second son, Ashot known by his moniker Kekela ("the Fair"), died in 867. This must have occurred in Adarnase's lifetime as 
2 CONC Ashot is not listed among his heirs.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The numbering of successive rulers in the early Bagratid period is very confused in that it moves between the different branches of the family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Suny, Ronald Grigor (1994), The Making of the Georgian Nation, p. 30. Indiana University Press, ISBN 0253209153 
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Toumanoff, Cyril (1967). Studies in Christian Caucasian History, p. 453. Georgetown University Press. 
