0 @N6000000002187624135@ NOTE Ptolemy VI Philometor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy VI Philometor (Greek: ?t??eµa??? F???µ?t??, c. 191-145 BC) was a king of Egypt from the Ptolemaic period. He reigned from 180 to 145 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy succeeded in 180 at the age of about 12 and ruled jointly with his mother, Cleopatra I, until her death in 176 BC. The following year he married his sister, Cleopatra II.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 170 BC, Antiochus IV began the sixth Syrian War and invaded Egypt twice. He was crowned as its king in 168, but abandoned his claim on the orders of the Roman Senate. From 169-164 Egypt was ruled by a triumvirate consisting of 
1 CONC Ptolemy, his sister-queen and his younger brother known as Ptolemy VIII Physcon, but in 164 he was driven out by his brother and went to Rome to seek support, which he received from Cato. He was restored the following year by the 
1 CONC intervention of the Alexandrians and ruled uneasily, cruelly suppressing frequent rebellions. In 152 BC he briefly ruled jointly with one of his sons, known as Ptolemy Eupator, but it is thought that Ptolemy Eupator died that same
1 CONC  year.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is reputed to have taken as eromenos a boy named Galestes, whom he loved not only for his good looks but also for his wisdom. (Aelian, Varia Historia, I.30) Ptolemy VI Philometer was killed in Syria, fighting against Alexander 
1 CONC Balas.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy Philometor reigned for a short time with his son, Ptolemy Eupator. Eupator is only attested on monumental inscriptions, and when he was first discovered, there was a theory that he was an elder brother of Philometor, and r
1 CONC eigned before him. As a result, some nineteenth century texts count Ptolemy Philometor as Ptolemy VII, and increase the numbers of all the later Ptolemies until "Ptolemy XVI Caesarion". The epithets, which have come down from anti
1 CONC quity, are unchanged.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ptolemy Philometor at LacusCurtius — (Chapter IX of E. R. Bevan's House of Ptolemy, 1923)
1 CONT     * Ptolemy VI — (Egyptian Royal Genealogy)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy V and Cleopatra I Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT First Reign
1 CONT with Cleopatra I, Cleopatra II, and Ptolemy VIII Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT Second Reign
1 CONT with Cleopatra I, Cleopatra II, Ptolemy VIII, and Ptolemy VII Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra II and Ptolemy VII
0 @N6000000002187624124@ NOTE Cleopatra II of Egypt
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra II (Greek: ??e?p?t?a, c. 185 BC - 116) was a queen (and briefly sole ruler) of Ptolemaic Egypt, and the daughter of Ptolemy V and Cleopatra I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the death of their mother (175), she was married to her brother in 173 BC, Ptolemy VI. They and their brother, Ptolemy VIII, were co-rulers of Egypt from 171 BC to 164.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 170, Antiochus IV of Syria invaded Egypt, resulting in a war which Egypt eventually won with Rome's help in 168.
1 CONT
1 CONT She became regent for her son Ptolemy VII on her husband's death in 145, and married her other brother, Ptolemy VIII Euergetes II "Physcon" the next year, whereupon Physcon slew his nephew/stepson and made himself king. In 142 he 
1 CONC took her younger daughter, his niece, Cleopatra III, as wife without divorcing his sister and made his new wife joint ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra II led a rebellion against Physcon in 131, and successfully drove him and Cleopatra III out of Egypt. She then proclaimed Physcon's 12-year-old son, Ptolemy Memphitis, as King, and herself as coregent (though she technic
1 CONC ally was the sole ruler at this point). However, Physcon managed to have Mephitis killed and sent the pieces of his body to Cleopatra, and she remained sole ruler of Egypt. She offered the throne to Demetrius II of Syria, but he w
1 CONC as assassinated by Physcon, and Cleopatra left for Syria in 127 BC. Physcon then returned to Egypt and reclaimed the throne. A public reconciliation of Cleopatra and Physcon was declared in 124. After this she ruled jointly with h
1 CONC er brother and daughter until 116 when Ptolemy died, leaving the kingdom to Cleopatra III. She herself died shortly after.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VI Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT First Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VI and Ptolemy VIII Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT Second Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VI, Ptolemy VII, Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra III Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra III
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT Third Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra III Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy IX and Cleopatra III
0 @N6000000001353854685@ NOTE Event: Ruled 170 - 164 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt [Alexandria] [co-regent with Ptolemy VI] 1
1 CONT Event: Ruled 145 - 116 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt 2
1 CONT Event: Ruled 163 - 145 BC, King of Cyrenaica [Libya] 1
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII EUERGETES II (Greek: "Benefactor II"), also called PHYSCON (Greek: "Potbellied") (d. 116 BC), Macedonian king of Egypt who played a divisive role in trying to win the kingship, making himself subservient to Rome and e
1 CONC ncouraging Roman interference in Egypt.
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII ruled jointly with his brother, Ptolemy VI Philometor, in 170-164 BC and alone during the next year; he was king of Cyrenaica(in modern Libya) in 163-145, and sole ruler of Egypt from 145 to hisdeath in 116, except fo
1 CONC r a brief exile in 131-129. Continuously quarreling with his queen, Cleopatra II, the widow of Philometor, he causedcivil war and economic collapse in Egypt. Late in his reign (118) he instituted extensive reforms to restore the c
1 CONC ountry.
1 CONT Around 117 an expedition sponsored by Ptolemy completed the first seavoyage to India via the Red Sea and Indian Ocean, beginning Egypt's interest in the spice trade. [Encyclopædia Britannica CD '97, PTOLEMY VIII EUERGETES II]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII Euergetes II, repulsive and nicknamed 'physon' [potbelly], was captivated by his niece, also Cleopatra, the daughter of his sister-wife Cleopatra. The niece agreed to the liaison so long as she could also become queen
1 CONC  -- so mother and daughter, sister and niece of Euergetes, became joint queens as Cleopatra II and III...The former wasmuch beloved by the people since her late husband Ptolemy VI's reign was such a shining example and memory comp
1 CONC ared to their present situation. Public resentment against Ptolemy VIII grew to such a point that he fled to Cyprus, taking the younger Cleopatra (III), their two children and the young boy Memphites (his son by Cleopatra II) with
1 CONC  him. His flight was not a moment too soon, for the mob broke into the palaceseeking his blood.
1 CONT In Cyprus, Euergetes plotted his return to Egypt where his sister, Cleopatra II, reigned as Cleopatra Philometor Soteira. In a fit of maniacal revenge against his sister and the Alexandrian mob which had been busy destroying his s
1 CONC tatues and memories of him, he murdered Memphites, his own son by Cleopatra II, and sent the child's dismembered body to her as a present on her birthday.
1 CONT In 129, now strong enough to invade Egypt, Euergetes returned from Cyprus and in 128 Cleopatra II fled for protection to her daughter, Cleopatra Thea, now married to Demetrius II of Syria. Strangely, she was to return to Egypt, an
1 CONC d Euergetes survived until 116. What happened tohis sister-wife Cleopatra II after her return is not known, but she presumably predeceased him as her daughter, Cleopatra III, inherited Egypt by Euergetes' will.
0 @N6000000001355636680@ NOTE Event: Ruled 170 - 164 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt [Alexandria] [co-regent with Ptolemy VI] 1
1 CONT Event: Ruled 145 - 116 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt 2
