0 @N6000000002187622293@ NOTE Mithridates IV of Pontus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates IV Philopator Philadelphus (in Greek M????d?t?? F??op?t?? F???de?fo?; died c. 150 BC), sixth king of Pontus and son of Mithridates III. The date of his accession to the throne is utterly mysterious, since we first hear
1 CONC  of him as ruler only in 154 BC, when he is mentioned as sending an auxiliary force to the assistance of Attalus II against Prusias II, king of Bithynia -- an important event, since it signaled the start of a policy of friendship 
1 CONC of Pontus wih Rome and its allies which would continue till Mithridates VI Eupator1 The only other mention we have of him is as much as twenty-five years before (179 BC): his name is associated with that of his brother Pharnaces i
1 CONC n the treaty concluded by the latter with the king of Pergamon Eumenes II, in a manner that would lead one to suppose he was already admitted to some share in the sovereign power.2
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Pharnaces I King of Pontus
1 CONT c. 155 BC – c. 150 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mithridates V
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hazel, John; Who's Who in the Roman World, "Mithridates IV", (2002)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Polybius, Histories, xxxiii. 12
1 CONT 2 Ibid., xxv. 2
0 @N6000000002187617506@ NOTE Ariarathes VI of Cappadocia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ariarathes VI Epiphanes Philopator (in Greek A??a????? Ep?fa??? F??op?t??; reigned 130–116 BC), king of Cappadocia, was the youngest son of Ariarathes V. He reigned about 14 years (130–116 BC). He was a child at his succession, an
1 CONC d for this reason the power was kept by his mother Nysa. At some point her mother seems to have poisoned all Ariarathes five brothers; but the infant king was saved by people loyal to the dynasty and Nysa killed. These facts were 
1 CONC a good pretext for Mithridates Euergetes (151–120 BC), king of Pontus, for trying to assert control over the country. For this he married Ariarathes to Laodice, Mithridates daughter; but since this wasn't deemed enough to trasform
1 CONC  Cappadocia in a satellite of Pontus, Mithridates Euergetes' son, Mithridates VI, murdered Ariarathes using Gordius, a Cappadocian nobleman.1 On his death the kingdom was briefly ruled by Ariarathes' widow and then seized by Nicom
1 CONC edes III, king of Bithynia, who married Laodice the widow of the late king, Laodice. But Nicomedes III was soon expelled by Mithridates VI, who placed upon the throne Ariarathes VII, a son of Ariarathes VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ariarathes V King of Cappadocia
1 CONT 130 BC – 116 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Ariarathes VII
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hazel, John; Who's Who in the Greek World, "Ariarathes VI", (1999)
1 CONT     * Head, Barclay; Historia Numorum, "Cappadocia", (1911)
1 CONT     * Justin; Epitome of Pompeius Trogus, John Selby Watson (translator); London, (1886)
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Ariarathes VI", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Note
