0 @N6000000002187718937@ NOTE Demetrius II Nicator
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Demetrius II of Syria)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius II (d. 125 BC), called Nicator (Victor), was the middle son of Demetrius I Soter. As a young boy, he fled to Crete after the death of his father, his mother and his older brother, when Alexander Balas usurped the Seleuci
1 CONC d throne. About 147 BC he returned to Syria, and with the help of Ptolemy VI Philometor, king of Egypt, regained his father's throne. The Egyptian king also divorced his daughter Cleopatra Thea from Balas and remarried her to Deme
1 CONC trius. Alexander fled to Cilicia.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Demetrius was not a popular king. The people of Syria had little respect for the young boy, who had come to power with the help of Egypt and Cretan mercenaries led by the ruthless condottier Lasthenes. The Antiochenians o
1 CONC ffered the Seleucid throne to Ptolemy VI, who had already conquered most of southern Syria for his own interest. However, he insisted Demetrius would become king, knowing that Rome would never tolerate a unified hellenistic state,
1 CONC  and the year after Ptolemy VI was killed when Alexander Balas made a last desperate attempt to regain his throne. The Egyptian troops marched home, leaderless and disillusioned, and with Balas dead as well Demetrius became sole m
1 CONC aster of the Seleucid kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT But new troubles soon arose. The pillaging of the Cretan soldiers caused the Antiochenians to rise in rebellion, and only after terrible massacres could order be restored. Soon after, the general Diodotus conquered Antioch and had
1 CONC  his protegé Antiochus VI Dionysus, the infant son of Alexander Balas, proclaimed king. Demetrius proved unable to retake the capital,instead establishing himself in Seleucia. Diodotus had Antiochus VI deposed a few years later, a
1 CONC nd made himself King as Tryphon, but the division of the kingdom between the legitimate Seleucid heir and the usurper in Antioch persisted.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Defeat and captivity
1 CONT In 139 BCE Parthian activity forced Demetrius to take action. He marched against Mithradates I, king of Parthia and was initially successful, but was defeated in the Iranian mountains and taken prisoner the following year. The Bab
1 CONC ylonian province of the Seleucid empire became Parthian, but in Syria was the dynasty's grip was reassured under Antiochus VII Sidetes, the younger brother of Demetrius, who also married Cleopatra Thea.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Mithradates had kept Demetrius II alive and even married him to a Parthian princess named Rhodogune, with whom he had children. However, Demetrius was restless and twice tried to escape from his exile on the shores of the Cas
1 CONC pian sea, once with the help of his friend Kallimander, who had gone to great lengths to rescue the king: he had travelled incognito through Babylonia and Parthia. When the two friends were captured, the Parthian king did not puni
1 CONC sh Kallimander but rewarded him for his fidelity to Demetrius. The second time Demetrius was captured when he tried to escape, Mithradates humiliated him by giving him a golden set of dice, thus hinting that Demetrius II was a res
1 CONC tless child who needed toys. It was however for political reasons that the Parthians treated Demetrius II kindly.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 130 BCE Antiochus Sidetes felt secure enough to march against Parthia, and scored massive initial successes. Now Mithradates released what he thought was a powerful move: he released Demetrius, hoping that the two brothers woul
1 CONC d start a civil war. However, Sidetes was defeated soon after his brother's release and never met him. Mithradates set people to pursue Demetrius, but he could return safely home to Syria to regain his throne and his queen as well
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT A failed second reign
1 CONT However, the Seleucid kingdom was now but a shadow of its former glory, and Demetrius had a hard time to rule even in Syria. Recollections of his cruelties and vices - along with his humiliating defeat - caused him to be greatly d
1 CONC etested. The Egyptian queen Cleopatra II set up an army for Demetrius, hoping to engage him in her civil wars against her brother king Ptolemy VIII, but this only added to his grief. The troops soon deserted, and king Ptolemy VIII
1 CONC  reacted by setting up yet another usurper, a man named Alexander II Zabinas against Demetrius.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 126 BCE Demetrius was defeated in a battle at Damascus, and killed on a ship near Tyre, after his wife Cleopatra Thea had deserted him. His miserable death - he was captured and possibly tortured - was a fitting epitaph to the 
1 CONC many shortcomings of his reign. Demetrius II was certainly uncapable to handle the developing threats to the Seleucid empire, but his reputation of cruelty was probably undeserved. He was only around fourteen at his coronation, an
1 CONC d the real power was in the hands of others.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his queen Cleopatra Thea and then by two of their sons, Seleucus V Philometor and Antiochus VIII Grypus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alexander Balas Seleucid King
1 CONT 146–139 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antiochus VII Sidetes 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Antiochus VII Sidetes Seleucid King
1 CONT 129–126 BCE Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra Thea
1 CONT and
1 CONT Seleucus V Philometor 
0 @N6000000002187624463@ NOTE Cleopatra Thea
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra Thea (c. 164 BC - 121 BC) surnamed Euergetis (i.e., "Benefactress") was a daughter of Ptolemy VI of Egypt and Cleopatra II of Egypt. She ruled Seleucid Syria from 125 BC to 121 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Alexander Balas in about 150 BC. This union produced Antiochus VI Dionysus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her second marriage was to Demetrius II Nicator in about 148 BC. Their children were Seleucus V Philometor, Antiochus VIII Grypus, and possibly a daughter (Laodice?). Demetrius was captured fighting against the Parthians and was c
1 CONC onsequently off the scene for a while.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her third husband, married during Demetrius' captivity, was his brother Antiochus VII Sidetes. They certainly had Antiochus IX Cyzicenus, but the names of any other children are uncertain. In about 129 BC, Antiochus was killed fig
1 CONC hting the Parthians. Demetrius had been released and returned home to reclaim his throne and queen. Cleopatra had taken the precaution of sending Antiochus IX (her son by Antiochus VII) to Cyzicus in Asia Minor (hence his surname)
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius unsuccessfully attempted to invade Egypt. In retaliation, the ruler of Egypt put forward Alexander Zabinas, allegedly a son of Alexander Balas as king of Syria, provoking civil war. After a defeat by Zabinas' forces at D
1 CONC amascus, Demetrius retreated to Ptolemais Hermiou only to find the city gates closed against him by Cleopatra. He boarded a ship to flee, but was killed on Cleopatra's orders.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 125 BC to 121 BC, Cleopatra ruled Syria, killing Demetrius' eldest son Seleucus when he attempted to claim the throne. To legitimize her reign, she shared the throne with her son Antiochus VIII Grypus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus became less controllable as he grew up and in 121 BC, she decided to eliminate him. As he returned from a hunt one day, she offered him a cup of wine. Since this was not common behavior for her, Antiochus was suspicious 
1 CONC and forced her to drink the wine, with the predictable outcome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alexander II Seleucid Ruler Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antiochus VIII 
0 @N6000000002187624086@ NOTE Cleopatra III of Egypt
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra III (Greek: ??e?p?t?a, 161–101 BC) was queen of Egypt 142–101 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was born in 161 BC to Ptolemy VI and Cleopatra II of Egypt. Ptolemy VII Neos Philopater was her brother. After the death of her father, her brother became the King of Egypt, and her mother was regent. In an attempt to gain con
1 CONC trol of Cleopatra II, Ptolemy VIII Euergetes II "Physcon" married her. Physcon later had Philopater killed, thus taking the throne for himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT He then discarded Cleopatra II and married Cleopatra III in 142 BC, making her Queen and joint ruler. His reign after that was peaceful, but he was very despotic. She bore Physcon these children: Ptolemy IX Philometor Soter II Lat
1 CONC hyros, Ptolemy X Alexander I, Cleopatra IV, Cleopatra V Tryphaena, and Cleopatra Selene.
1 CONT
1 CONT When he died, he left the throne to Cleopatra and whichever son she wished. If it were up to her, she would have chosen her younger son Alexander, but the Alexandrians forced her to bring Lathyros from Cyprus, of which he was gove
1 CONC rnor, to co-rule, becoming Ptolemy IX. The younger son Alexander was then sent to Cyprus to fill Lathyros's former position.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually, by accusing Lathyros of murdering her, she successfully deposed him and brought Alexander back to Egypt, becoming Ptolemy X. However, she eventually tired of Alexander and brought back Lathyros. Again, she grew tired o
1 CONC f Lathyros as well, and Alexander was brought back. Tiring of his mother's intrigue, Alexander had her assassinated in 101 BC.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra II Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT First Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra II Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra II
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra II Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT Second Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VIII, Cleopatra II, Ptolemy IX, and Ptolemy X Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy X
0 @N6000000002187991280@ NOTE Ptolemy VII Neos Philopator
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy VII Neos Philopator (Greek: ?t??eµa??? ???? F???p?t??) was an Egyptian king of the Ptolemaic period. His reign is controversial, and it is possible that he did not reign at all, but was only granted royal dignity posthumou
1 CONC sly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Even his identity is unclear. According to one reconstruction, he was the son of Ptolemy VI Philometor and Cleopatra II of Egypt, he reigned briefly with his father in 145 BC, and for a short time after that, and was murdered by h
1 CONC is uncle, Ptolemy VIII Physcon, who succeeded him. Alternately, some scholars identify Ptolemy Neos Philopator with Ptolemy Memphites, a son of Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra II who was murdered by his father; or else point to a numbe
1 CONC r of minor co-regents - all of whom were named Ptolemy as was the tradition in the dynasty. By tradition, though, the numbering of the Ptolemies is kept intact. (Occasionally, the numbering is reversed, and Ptolemy VIII Physcon is
1 CONC  numbered as Ptolemy VII, with the boy-king Neos Philopator numbered Ptolemy VIII; in some older sources, Ptolemy VII is omitted altogether. This lowers the numbering of all the later Ptolemies, until Caesarion is Ptolemy XIV; the
1 CONC  nicknames are unchanged, of course.)
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VI Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT with Ptolemy VI and Cleopatra II Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra II
0 @N6000000002187624035@ NOTE Ptolemy IX Lathyros
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy IX Soter II or Lathyros ("chickpea") was king of Egypt three times, from 116 BC to 110 BC, 109 BC to 107 BC and 88 BC to 81 BC, with intervening periods ruled by his brother, Ptolemy X Alexander.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first he was chosen by his mother Cleopatra III to be her co-regent (his father Ptolemy VIII wished that she would rule with one of her sons), though she was more forced to choose him by the Alexandrians. He married his sister 
1 CONC Cleopatra IV, but his mother pushed her out and replaced her with his younger sister Cleopatra Selene. Later, she claimed that he tried to kill her, and successfully deposed him, putting her favorite son Alexander on the throne as
1 CONC  co-regent with her. However, she later grew tired of the now Ptolemy X and deposed him, putting Ptolemy IX back on the throne. She was soon murdered by Ptolemy X, who took the throne again. He was then killed in battle, and Ptole
1 CONC my IX reigned until his own death.
1 CONT
1 CONT His daughter Berenice III took the throne after his death, and reigned for about a year. She was forced to marry her stepson Alexander, who reigned under the name Ptolemy XI and had her killed 19 days later.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy IX's name recalls that of his great ancestor, Ptolemy I Soter; note that in older references, he may be numbered VIII.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External Link
1 CONT Ptolemy Soter II at LacusCurtius — (Chapter XI of E. R Bevan's House of Ptolemy, 1923) 
