0 @N6000000002187718918@ NOTE Antiochus VIII Grypus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus VIII Epiphanes/Callinicus/Philometor, nicknamed Grypus (hook-nose) was son of Demetrius II Nicator and was crowned as a boy in 125 BC after his mother Cleopatra Thea had killed his elder brother Seleucus V Philometor, ru
1 CONC ling jointly with her. After Antiochus defeated usurper Alexander II Zabinas in 123 BC his mother tried to poison him with wine, but the suspicious king forced her to drink the cup herself. (The story may have been inspired by the
1 CONC  fact that Grypus was interested in toxicology; some poems about poisonous herbs believed to have been written by him are quoted by the famous physician Galen.)
1 CONT
1 CONT He married the Ptolemaic princess Tryphaena, but in 116 BC his half-brother and cousin Antiochus IX Cyzicenus (see Antiochus VII Sidetes) returned from exile and a civil war began. Cyzicenus' wife, also named Cleopatra, was a half
1 CONC -sister of Tryphaena and was eventually killed in a dramatic fashion in the temple of Daphne outside Antioch, on the order of Tryphaena. Cyzicenus eventually killed Tryphaena as revenge. The two brothers then divided Syria between
1 CONC  them until Grypus was killed by his minister Heracleon in 96 BC. Five of his sons succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite political shortcomings, Grypus was a popular king. His ugly, lazy appearance on coins (common among the last Seleucids), together with stories of his lavish banquets, made posterity believe his dynasty was degenerated and 
1 CONC decadent. This was however a conscious image, an invocation of the hellenistic idea Tryphe - meaning good life, which the last Seleucids strove to be associated with, as opposed to the exhausting civil wars and feuds which trouble
1 CONC d their reigns in reality.
1 CONT
1 CONT A story of his luxorious parties claims he sent food home with guests whom attended banquets, complete with a camel as beast of burden, as well as an with attendant to carry the guest himself. This should certainly have caused som
1 CONC e strain on the already depleted treasury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra Seleucid Ruler Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antiochus IX 
0 @N6000000002187624015@ NOTE Cleopatra V of Egypt
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra V of Egypt (Greek: ??e?p?t?a) was the mother of Cleopatra VII, by her husband and brother Ptolemy XII of Egypt, and was possibly the mother of Cleopatra VI of Egypt and Berenice IV. She co-ruled Egypt with her daughter B
1 CONC erenice IV for a year before her death, when her daughter reigned alone in Egypt.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Genealogy of Ptolemaic Dynasty
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy XII Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT with Ptolemy XII and Berenice IV Succeeded by:
1 CONT Berenice IV
0 @N6000000002187624004@ NOTE Ptolemy XII Auletes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT     Numbering the Ptolemies is a modern invention; the Greeks distinguished them by nickname. The number given here is the present consensus; but there has been some disagreement about which Ptolemies should be counted as reigning
1 CONC . Older sources may give a number one higher or lower, but the same epithet.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy XII Neos Dionysos Theos Philopator Theos Philadelphos (Greek: ?t??eµa??? ???? ?????s?? T??? F???p?t?? Te?? F???de?f??, New Dionysus, God Beloved of his Father, God Beloved of his Brother) (117 BC – 51 BC) was son of Ptolem
1 CONC y IX Soter II. His mother is unknown. He was king of Egypt from 80 BC to 58 BC and from 55 BC until his death in 51 BC. He was more commonly known as "Auletes" (The Flute Player), or "Nothos" (The Bastard).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 80 BC, Ptolemy XII succeeded Ptolemy XI to the throne of Egypt. The latter had been lynched by an angry crowd, after he had killed his popular coregent Berenice III, who was incidentally also a daughter of Ptolemy IX Soter II. 
1 CONC When Ptolemy XI died without a male heir, the only available male descendents of the Ptolemy I lineage were the illegitimate sons of Ptolemy IX by an unknown Alexandrian Greek concubine (Clayton, 1994). The boys were living in exi
1 CONC le in Sinope, at the court of Mithridates VI, King of Pontus. The eldest of the boys was proclaimed king as Ptolemy XII Neos Dionysos and married his sister, Tryphaena.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first, Ptolemy XII was coregent with his sister Cleopatra VI Tryphaina and his wife Cleopatra V Tryphaena, but the former mysteriously disappears from the records in 69 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy XII's personal cult name (Neos Dionysos) earned him the ridiculing sobriquet Auletes (flute player) — as we learn from Strabo's writing (Strabo XVII, 1, 11):
1 CONT
1 CONT Now all at kings after the third Ptolemy, being corrupted by luxurious living, have administered the affairs of government badly, but worst of all the fourth, seventh, and the last, Auletes, who, apart from his general licentiousn
1 CONC ess, practised the accompaniment of choruses with the flute, and upon this he prided himself so much that he would not hesitate to celebrate contests in the royal palace, and at these contests would come forward to vie with the op
1 CONC posing contestants.
1 CONT
1 CONT During his reign, Ptolemy XII tried to secure his own fate and the fate of his dynasty by means of a pro-Roman policy. At the height of his success in 59 BC, after paying substantial bribes to Julius Caesar and Pompey, a formal al
1 CONC liance was formed (foedera) and his name was inscribed into the list of friends and allies of the people of Rome (amici et socii populi Romani). However in 58 BC after he failed to comment on the Roman conquest of Cyprus, a territ
1 CONC ory ruled by his brother, he was forced to flee to Rome. His daughter Berenice IV became his successor. From Rome he prosecuted his restitution. He finally recovered his throne by paying Aulus Gabinius 10,000 talents to invade Egy
1 CONC pt in 55 BC. From then on he reigned until he fell ill in 51 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon before his death he chose his daughter Cleopatra VII as his coregent. In his will he declared that she and her brother Ptolemy XIII should rule the kingdom together. To safeguard his interests, he made the people of Rome exec
1 CONC utors of his will.
1 CONT
1 CONT Auletes means pipes-player, and refers to his chubby cheeks (a Ptolemaic family trait ), like the inflated cheeks of a pipe-player.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Further reading
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Clayton, A. (1994) "Chronicle of the Pharaohs: The Reign by Reign Record of the Rulers and Dynasties of Ancient Egypt." London: Thames and Hudson. ISBN 0-500-05074-0.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Strabo Geographika Books 1-7, 15-17 in English translation, ed. H. L. Jones (1924), at LacusCurtius
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy XI Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT First Reign
1 CONT with Cleopatra V and Cleopatra VI Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra V and Berenice IV
1 CONT Berenice IV Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT Second Reign
1 CONT with Cleopatra VII Ptolemy XIII and Cleopatra VII
0 @N6000000002187991205@ NOTE Ptolemy XI Alexander II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT     Numbering the Ptolemies is a modern invention; the Greeks distinguished them by nickname. The number given here is the present consensus; but there has been some disagreement about which Ptolemies should be counted as reigning
1 CONC . Older sources may give a number one higher or lower, but the same epithet.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy XI Alexander II was a member of the Ptolemaic dynasty who ruled Egypt for a few days in 80 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy XI was born to Ptolemy X Alexander and either Cleopatra Selene or Berenice III. Ptolemy IX Lathryos died in 81 or 80, leaving no legitimate heir, and so Cleopatra Berenice (another name of Berenice III) ruled alone for a t
1 CONC ime. However, Rome's Sulla wanted a pro-Roman ruler on the throne, and sent the young son of Ptolemy X to Egypt, displaying Ptolemy Alexander's will in Rome as justification for this obvious intervention.
1 CONT
1 CONT The will also required Ptolemy XI to marry Cleopatra Berenice, who was his stepmother (or possibly his natural mother - the ancient sources are unclear). However, nineteen days after the marriage, Ptolemy murdered his bride for un
1 CONC known reasons, an unwise move since Berenice was very popular; Ptolemy was immediately lynched by the citizens of Alexandria.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by Ptolemy XII.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Peter Green, Alexander to Actium (University of California Press, 1990), pp. 553-554 ISBN 0-520-05611-6
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Berenice III Ptolemaic King of Egypt
1 CONT with Berenice III Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy XII
0 @N6000000002187600784@ NOTE Darius II of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Darius II, originally called Ochus and often surnamed Nothus (from Greek ?????, meaning 'bastard'), was emperor of Persia from 423 BC to 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes I, who died shortly after December 24, 424 BC, was followed by his son Xerxes II. After a month and a half Xerxes was murdered by his brother Secydianus or Sogdianus (the form of the name is uncertain). His illegitimate
1 CONC  brother, Ochus, satrap of Hyrcania, rebelled against Sogdianus, and after a short fight killed him, and suppressed by treachery the attempt of his own brother Arsites to imitate his example. Ochus adopted the name Darius (in the 
1 CONC chronicles he is called Nothos, meaning "the bastard"). Neither Xerxes II nor Secydianus occurs in the dates of the numerous Babylonian tablets from Nippur; here the reign of Darius II follows immediately after that of Artaxerxes 
1 CONC I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Darius's reign we know very little (a rebellion of the Medes in 409 BC is mentioned in Xenophon), except that he was quite dependent on his wife Parysatis. In the excerpts from Ctesias some harem intrigues are recorded, in whic
1 CONC h he played a disreputable part. As long as the power of Athens remained intact he did not meddle in Greek affairs; even the support which the Athenians in 413 BC gave to the rebel Amorges in Caria would not have roused him, had n
1 CONC ot the Athenian power been broken in the same year before Syracuse. He gave orders to his satraps in Asia Minor, Tissaphernes and Pharnabazus, to send in the overdue tribute of the Greek towns, and to begin a war with Athens; for 
1 CONC this purpose they entered into an alliance with Sparta. In 408 BC he sent his son Cyrus to Asia Minor, to carry on the war with greater energy. In 404 BC Darius II died after a reign of nineteen years, and was followed by Artaxerx
1 CONC es II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Based on an article from a 1911 encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sogdianus Great King of Media and Persia
1 CONT 423–404 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes II 
1 CONT King of Egypt
1 CONT 423–404 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amyrtaeus 
0 @N6000000002187600795@ NOTE Parysatis
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Parysatis was the illegitimate daughter of Artaxerxes I, Emperor of Persia and Andia of Babylon. She was the half-sister of Xerxes II, Sogdianus and Darius II. She married her half-brother Darius and had two sons, Artaxerxes III a
1 CONC nd Cyrus the Younger. When her husband died, she supported her younger son Cyrus. When Cyrus was defeated in the Battle of Cunaxa she blamed the starpt Tissapharnes for the death of her son. She later had Tissapharnes assassainate
1 CONC d.
0 @N6000000002187600822@ NOTE Died: 3 Sept 401 in battle of Kounaxa
