0 @N6000000002187617655@ NOTE Ariobarzanes II of Cius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ariobarzanes (in Greek A??oßa??????; ruled 363–337 BC) succeeded his father, Mithridates, as ruler of the Greek town of Cius in Mysia, governing 26 years between 363 and 337 BC for count of the Persians.1 He appears to have held s
1 CONC ome high office in the Persian court five years before the death of his father, as we find him, apparently on behalf of the king, sending an embassy to Greece in 368.2 Ariobarzanes, who is called by Diodorus3 satrap of Phrygia, an
1 CONC d by Nepos4 satrap of Lydia, Ionia, and Phrygia, revolted from Artaxerxes II in 362. Demosthenes, in 352, speaks of Ariobarzanes and his three sons having been lately made Athenian citizens.5 He mentions him again6 in the followin
1 CONC g year, 351, and says, that the Athenians had sent Timotheus to his assistance; but that when the Athenian general saw that Ariobarzanes was in open revolt against the king, he refused to assist him. He was succeeded as governor o
1 CONC f Cius by Mithridates, possibly his son, certainly a relative.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
