0 @N6000000002187622240@ NOTE Alexander I of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alexander I (Greek: ????a?d??? ? ?a?ed??) was ruler of Macedon from 498 BC to 454 BC. He was the son of Amyntas I of Macedon.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Herodotus he was unfriendly to Persia, and had the envoys of Darius I killed when they arrived at the court of his father during the Ionian Revolt. However, he was forced to submit to Persia during the invasion of Gre
1 CONC ece by Darius' son Xerxes I, and he acted as a representative of the Persian governor Mardonius during peace negotiations after the Persian defeat at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BC. Despite his cooperation with Persia, he frequen
1 CONC tly gave supplies and advice to the Greeks, and warned them of Mardonius' plans before the Battle of Plataea in 479 BC. Alexander eventually regained Macedonia's independence after the end of the Persian Wars.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Macedon was considered a semi-barbaric state by some Greeks (especially those whose colonies near Macedonia were threatened by Macedonian expansion), Alexander claimed descent from Argive Greeks and Hercules. After a cour
1 CONC t of hellanodikai determined his claim to be true, he was permitted to participate in the Olympic Games, an honor reserved only for Greeks. He modeled his court after Athens and was a patron of the poet Pindar.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 450 he was succeeded by his son Perdiccas II.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Amyntas I King of Macedon
1 CONT 498 BC–454 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Perdiccas II
