0 @N6000000002187624501@ NOTE Phraates V of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Phraates V of Parthia, known as Phraataces (a diminutive), ruled the Parthian Empire from 2 BC to AD 4. He was the younger son of Phraates IV of Parthia (37–2 BC) and the "goddess Musa", with whom he is associated on his coins. Un
1 CONC der Phraates V a war threatened to break out with Rome about the supremacy in Armenia and Media. But when Augustus (27 BC – AD 14) sent his adopted son Gaius Caesar into the east in order to invade Parthia, the Parthians preferred
1 CONC  to conclude a treaty (AD 1), by which once again Armenia was recognized as in the Roman sphere. Soon afterwards Phraates V and his mother were slain by the Parthians, about 4. Josephus alleges that Phraates V married his mother M
1 CONC usa, and, this being unacceptable to the Parthians, they rose up and overthrew him, offering the crown to Orodes III of Parthia (who ruled briefly in 6).
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Phraates IV King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Orodes III 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews, xviii, 2. 
0 @N6000000002188110633@ NOTE Artabanus II of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Artabanus II of Parthia ruled the Parthian Empire from about AD 10 to 38. He was the son of a princess of the Arsacid Dynasty who lived in the East among the Dahan nomads. He was raised to the throne by those Parthian grandees who
1 CONC  would not acknowledge Vonones I, whom the Roman Emperor Augustus had sent from Rome (where he lived as hostage) as successor of his father Phraates IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT The war between the two pretenders was long and doubtful; on a coin Vonones mentions a victory over Artabanus. At last Artabanus defeated his rival completely and occupied the Parthian capital Ctesiphon. Vonones fled to Armenia, w
1 CONC here he was acknowledged as king, under the protection of the Romans. But when Artabanus invaded Armenia, Vonones fled to Syria, and the emperor Tiberius thought it prudent to support him no longer. Tiberius' nephew and heir Germa
1 CONC nicus, whom he sent to the East, concluded a treaty with Artabanus, in which he was recognized as king and friend of the Romans. Armenia was given in AD 18 to Zeno, the son of the king of Pontus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Artabanus, like all Parthian princes, was much troubled by the opposition of the grandees. He is said to have been very cruel in consequence of his education among the Dahan barbarians. To strengthen his power he killed all the Ar
1 CONC sacid princes whom he could reach. Rebellions of the subject nations may have occurred also. We learn that he intervened in the Greek city Seleucia on the Tigris in favour of the oligarchs, and that two Jewish brigands, Anilai and
1 CONC  Asinai, maintained themselves for years in Neerda in the swamps of Babylonia, and were acknowledged as dynasts by Artabanus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 35 he tried anew to conquer Armenia, and to establish his son Arsaces as king there. A war with Rome seemed inevitable. But that party among the Parthian magnates which was hostile to Artabanus applied to Tiberius for a king of
1 CONC  the race of Phraates. Tiberius sent Phraates's grandson, Tiridates III, and ordered Lucius Vitellius (the father of the emperor Vitellius) to restore the Roman authority in the East. By very dexterous military and diplomatic oper
1 CONC ations Vitellius succeeded completely. Artabanus was deserted by his followers and fled to the East. Tiridates, who was proclaimed king, could no longer maintain himself, because he appeared to be a vassal of the Romans; Artabanus
1 CONC  returned from Hyrcania with a strong army of Scythian (Dahan) auxiliaries, and was again acknowledged by the Parthians. Tiridates left Seleucia and fled to Syria. But Artabanus was not strong enough for a war with Rome; he theref
1 CONC ore concluded a treaty with Vitellius in 37, in which he gave up all further pretensions. A short time after-wards Artabanus was deposed again, and a certain Cinnamus was proclaimed king. Artabanus took refuge with his vassal, the
1 CONC  king Izates of Adiabene; and Izates by negotiations and the promise of a complete pardon induced the Parthians to restore Artabanus once more to the throne. Shortly afterwards Artabanus died, and was succeeded by his son, Vardane
1 CONC s, whose reign was still more turbulent than that of his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vonones I King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tiridates III 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Tiridates III King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vardanes I 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Tacitus, Annals, ii–vi. 
1 CONT Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews, 18–20. 
0 @N6000000002187624482@ NOTE Vonones I of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vonones I of Parthia (??O??S on his coins) ruled the Parthian Empire from about 8 to 12. He was the eldest son of Phraates IV of Parthia (ruled c. 37–2 BC) and was sent to Rome as a hostage in the 20s BC as surety for a treaty his
1 CONC  father made with Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the assassination of Orodes III in about 6, the Parthians applied to Augustus for a new King from the house of Arsaces. Augustus sent them Vonones I, but he could not maintain himself as King; he had been educated as a Roman
1 CONC , and was despised by the Parthian nobility as a slave of the Romans. Another member of the Arsacid house, Artabanus II (ruled c. 10–38), who was living among the Dahan nomads in the east of Parthia, was invited to the throne. In 
1 CONC a civil war he defeated and expelled Vonones I.
1 CONT
1 CONT The coins of Vonones I date from 8 to 12 and bear the inscription "King Vonones, conqueror of Artabanus" commemorating a temporary victory over his rival. Those of Artabanus II begin in 10. In about 12 Vonones I fled into Armenia 
1 CONC and became King there. But Artabanus II demanded his deposition, and as Augustus did not wish to begin a war with the Parthians he removed Vonones I into Syria, where he was kept in custody, though in a kingly style. Later he was 
1 CONC moved to Cilicia, and when he tried to escape in about 19, he was killed by his guards.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Orodes III King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artabanus II 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Hon. Ana. 5, 9. 
1 CONT Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews, xviii, 2, 4. 
1 CONT Tacitus, Annals, ii, 4, 58, 68. 
0 @N6000000002187624452@ NOTE Orodes III of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Orodes III of Parthia was raised to the throne of the Parthian Empire by the magnates after the death of Phraates V, c. AD 6. He was killed after a short reign "on account of his extreme cruelty" (Josephus). After his death, 
1 CONC Phraates' brother Vonones I tried to assume the thone, but a civil war with Artabanus II followed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
