0 @N6000000002188198831@ NOTE Amyntas III of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Amyntas III (or II), son of Arrhidaeus, grandfather of Alexander the Great, was king of Macedon from 393 (or 389) to 369 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT He came to the throne after the ten years of confusion which followed the death of Archelaus II, the patron of art and literature. But he had many enemies at home; in 383 he was driven out by the Illyrians, but in the following ye
1 CONC ar, with the aid of the Thessalians, he recovered his kingdom.
1 CONT
1 CONT He concluded a treaty with the Spartans, who assisted him to reduce Olynthus (379). He also entered into a league with Jason of Pherae, and assiduously cultivated the friendship of Athens.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his wife, Eurydice, he had three sons, the youngest of whom was the famous Philip II of Macedon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Argaeus II King of Macedon
1 CONT 393 BC–369 BC Succeeded by
1 CONT Alexander II
