1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Xenophon, Cyropaedia, viii. 8; Aristotle, Politics, v. 10
1 CONT 2 Xenophon, Hellenica, i. 4
1 CONT 3 Ibid., v. 1
0 @N6000000002187614739@ NOTE Esarhaddon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Esarhaddon (Greek and Biblical form; Akkadian Aššur-aha-iddina "Ashur has given a brother to me"), was a king of Assyria who reigned 681 BC-669 BC), the youngest son of Sennacherib and the Aramaic queen Naqi'a (Zakitu), Sennacheri
1 CONC b's second wife. When, despite being the youngest son, he was named successor by his father, his elder brothers tried to discredit him. Oracles had named Esarhaddon as the person to free the exiles and rebuild Babylon, the destruc
1 CONC tion of which by Sennacherib was felt to be sacrilegious. Esarhaddon remained crown prince, but was forced into exile at an unknown place beyond Hanilgalbat (Mitanni), that is, beyond the Euphrates, most likely somewhere in what i
1 CONC s now southeastern Turkey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sennacherib was murdered in 681 BC, some claim at the instigation of Esarhaddon, though this seems hardly likely, as he was not in a situation to exploit unrest arising from the death of his father. He returned to the capital of N
1 CONC ineveh in forced marches and defeated his rival brothers in six weeks of civil war. He was formally declared king in spring of 681 BC. His brothers fled the land, and their followers and families were put to death. In the same yea
1 CONC r he began the rebuilding of Babylon, including the well-known Esagila (sometimes identified with Tower of Babel). The statues of the Babylonian gods were restored and returned to the city. In order not to appear too biased in fav
1 CONC or of Babylonia, he ordered the reconstruction of the Assyrian sanctuary of Esharra in Ashur as well. Foreigners were forbidden to enter this temple. Both buildings were dedicated almost at the same date, in year two of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first military campaigns of Esarhaddon were directed against nomadic tribes of southern Mesopotamia, the Dakkuri and Gambulu, who had been harassing the peasants. In 679 BC the Cimmerians, who had already killed his grandfathe
1 CONC r Sargon, reappeared in Cilicia and Tabal under their new ruler Teushpa. Esarhaddon defeated them near Hubushna, and defeated the rebellious inhabitants of Hilakku as well. The Cimmerians withdrew to the west, where they were to d
1 CONC estroy the kingdom of Phrygia in 676, together with Scythian and Urartian help.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Sidonian king Abdi-Milkutti, who had risen up against the Assyrian king, was defeated in 677 BC and beheaded. The town of Sidon was destroyed and rebuilt as Kar-Ashur-aha-iddina, the Harbor of Esarhaddon. The population was de
1 CONC ported to Assyria. A share of the plunder went to the loyal king of rival Tyre. The partly conserved text of a treaty with Tyre mentions the kings of Judah, Edom, Moab, Gaza, Ashkelon, Ekron, Byblos, Arvad, Samsi-muruna, Ammon, As
1 CONC hdod, ten kings from the coast of the sea, and ten kings from the middle of the sea (usually identified with Cyprus), as Assyrian allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 676 BC Esarhaddon took the towns of Sissu and Kundu in the Taurus mountains. The Mannaeans, the Scythians under their king Ishpakaia, and the Guti of the Zagros proved to be a nuisance as well, as is attested by numerous oracle
1 CONC -texts. The Mannaeans, former vassals of the Assyrians, were no longer restricted to the area around Lake Urmia, but had spread into Zamua, where they interrupted the horse trade between Parsuash and Assyria and refused to pay fur
1 CONC ther tribute. After the fall of Phrygia, a daughter of Esarhaddon was wedded to the Scythian prince Partatua of Sakasene in order to improve relations with the nomads. The Medes under [Khshathrita (Phraortes) had been the target o
1 CONC f a campaign as well, the date of which is unclear (possibly before 676 BC). Later, Assyrian hosts reached the border of the "salt-desert" near the mountain Bikni, that is, near Teheran. A number of fortresses secured the Zagros: 
1 CONC Bit-Parnakki, Bit-kari and Harhar (Kar-Sharrukin).
1 CONT
1 CONT A certain Mugallu had taken possession of parts of Meliddu (Melitene), and associated himself with the king of Tabal. Meliddu was besieged in 675 BC, but without success. That same year, Humban-Haltash II of Elam began a campaign 
1 CONC against Sippar, but was defeated by the Babylonians, and died soon afterwards. His brother and successor Urtaki restored peace with Assyria.
1 CONT
1 CONT A preliminary campaign against Egypt begun by Esarhaddon the next year seems to have failed. Meanwhile, Esarhaddon was waging war in the land of Bazu, situated opposite of the island of Dilmun (Bahrain), probably Qatar, "where sna
1 CONC kes and scorpions cover the ground like ants" - a dry land of salt deserts. In 673 BC, Esarhaddon waged war against Urartu under king Rusas II, that had strengthened again after the ravages of Sargon and the Cimmerians.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 672 BC, crown prince Sin-iddina-apla died. He had been the oldest son and designated as king of Assyria, while the second son Shamash-shum-ukin was to become the ruler of Babylon. Now, the younger Assurbanipal became crown prin
1 CONC ce, but he was very unpopular with the court and the priesthood. Contracts were made with leading Assyrians, members of the royal family and foreign rulers, to assure their loyalty to the crown prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 671 BC Esarhaddon went to war against Pharaoh Taharqa of Egypt. Part of his army stayed behind to deal with rebellions in Tyre, and perhaps Ashkelon. The remainder went south to Rapihu, then crossed the Sinai, a desert inhabite
1 CONC d by dreadful and dangerous animals, and entered Egypt. In the summer he took Memphis, and Taharqa fled to Upper Egypt. Esarhaddon now called himself "king of Musur, Patros and Kush", and returned with rich booty from the cities o
1 CONC f the delta. Almost as soon as the king left, Egypt rebelled against Assyrian rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Esarhaddon had to contend with court intrigues at Nineveh that led to the execution of several nobles, and sent his general, Sha-Nabu-shu, to restore order in the Nile Valley. In 669 BC, he went to Egypt in person, but suddenly di
1 CONC ed in autumn of the same year, in Harran. He was succeeded by Assurbanipal as king of Assyria and Shamash-shum-ukin as king of Babylonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT In fiction
1 CONT Esarhaddon is also a major character in the novel The Assyrian, written as a narrative of a fictional half-brother to Esarhaddon, "Tiglath".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT A summary of Assyrian kings 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sennacherib King of Assyria
1 CONT 681–669 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashurbanipal 
1 CONT King of Babylon
1 CONT 681–669 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shamash-shum-ukin 
0 @N6000000002187614750@ NOTE Ashurbanipal
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashurbanipal (ah-shur-BA-neh-pal), Assurbanipal or Sardanapal, in Akkadian Aššur-bani-apli, (b. 685 BCE - d. 627 BCE) (reigned 669 - 627 BCE), the son of Esarhaddon and Naqi'a-Zakutu, was the last great king of ancient Assyria. He
1 CONC  is famous as one of the few kings in antiquity who could himself read and write. Assyrian sculpture reached its apogee under his rule (Northern palace and south-western palace at Nineveh, battle of Ulai). The Greeks knew him as S
1 CONC ardanapalos; Latin and other medieval texts refer to him as Sardanapalus. In the Bible he is called As(e)nappar or Osnapper (Ezra 4:10).
1 CONT
1 CONT During his rule, Assyrian splendour was not only visible in its military power, but also its culture and art. Ashurbanipal created "the first systematically collected library" at Nineveh, where he gathered all cuneiform literature
1 CONC  available by that time. A library was distinct from an archive: earlier repositories of documents had accumulated passively, in the course of administrative routine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Reign 
1 CONT 3 See also 
1 CONT 4 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT  Ashurbanipal was born toward the end of a fifteen-hundred-year period of Assyrian ascendancy. His name in Assyrian is Ashur-bani-apli (the god Ashur has made a[nother] son), affirming that he was not intended to stand in the line
1 CONC  of royal accession.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father, Esarhaddon, youngest son of Sennacherib, had become heir when the crown prince, Ashur-nadin-shumi, was deposed by rebels from his position as vassal for Babylon. Esarhaddon was not the son of Sennacherib's queen, Tashm
1 CONC etum-sharrat, but of the West Semitic "palace woman" Zakutu, known by her native name, Naqi'a. The only queen known for Esarhaddon was Ashur-hamat, who died in 672 BCE
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashurbanipal grew up in the small palace called bit reduti (house of succession), built by Sennacherib when he was crown prince in the northern quadrant of Nineveh. In 694, Sennacherib had completed the "Palace Without Rival" at t
1 CONC he southwest corner of the acropolis, obliterating most of the older structures. The "House of Succession" had become the palace of Esarhaddon, the crown prince. In this house, Ashurbanipal's grandfather was assassinated by uncles
1 CONC  identified only from the biblical account as Adrammelek and Sharezer. From this conspiracy, Esarhaddon emerged as king in 681. He proceeded to rebuild as his residence the bit masharti (weapons house, or arsenal). The "House of S
1 CONC uccession" was left to his mother and the younger children, including Ashurbanipal.
1 CONT
1 CONT The names of five brothers and one sister are known. Sin-iddin-apli, the intended crown prince, died prior to 672. Not having been expected to become heir to the throne, Ashurbanipal was trained in scholarly pursuits as well as th
1 CONC e usual horsemanship, hunting, chariotry, soldierliness, craftsmanship, and royal decorum. In a unique autobiographical statement, Ashurbanipal specified his youthful scholarly pursuits as having included oil divination, mathemati
1 CONC cs, and reading and writing. Ashurbanipal was the only Assyrian king who learned how to read and write.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 672, upon the death of his queen, Esarhaddon reorganized the line of succession at the instigation of his mother. He used the submission of Median chieftains to draft a treaty. The chieftains swore that if Esarhaddon died while
1 CONC  his sons were still minors, they and their children would guarantee the succession of Ashurbanipal as king of Assyria and Shamash-shum-ukin as king of Babylon. A monumental stela set up two years later in a northwestern province 
1 CONC portrays Esarhaddon in high relief upon its face and each of the sons on a side. These portraits, the earliest dated for Ashurbanipal and his brother, show both with the full beard of maturity.
1 CONT
1 CONT The princes pursued diverse educations thereafter. Extant letters from Shamash-shum-ukin offer his father reports of the situation in Babylon; Ashurbanipal at home received letters as crown prince. The situation came to an immedia
1 CONC te crisis in 669, when Esarhaddon, on campaign to Egypt, died suddenly. Ashurbanipal did not accede to the kingship of Assyria until late in the year. His grandmother required all to support his sole claim to the throne. The offic
1 CONC ial ceremonies of coronation came in the second month of the new year, and within the same year (668), Ashurbanipal installed his brother as King of Babylon. The transition took place smoothly, and the dual monarchy of the youthfu
1 CONC l brothers began. Texts describe their relationship as if they were twins. It was clear, however, that Ashurbanipal, as king of Assyria, like his fathers before him, was also "king of the universe."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT Tablets from the library of Nineveh preserve the most complete sources for both the Sumerian and Akkadian versions of the Epic of Gilgamesh. Other sets of tablets offer what is essentially a Sumerian-Akkadian dictionary. There are
1 CONC  arcane astronomical/astrological texts. By far, the largest group of tablets (almost all of which are in the British Museum, London) are 'omen' texts that taught the scribes how to recognize the significance of portents.
1 CONT
1 CONT Assurbanipal became crown prince in 672, when Esarhaddon's eldest son Sin-iddina-apla died. Assurbanipal was very unpopular with the court and the priesthood. Contracts were made with leading Assyrians, members of the royal family
1 CONC , and foreign rulers to assure their loyalty to the crown prince. But it was only the energy of his mother Naqi'a-Zakutu that assured his ascent to the throne when Esarhaddon suddenly died during his Egyptian campaign in 669.
1 CONT
1 CONT The early part of Ashurbanipal's reign, like that of most Assyrian kings, was marked by incessant warfare. He made war on his brother Shamash-shum-ukin, who had been installed as king in Babylon, and who had rebelled against him. 
1 CONC The Babylonian king was the leader of a large coalition of peoples from southern Mesopotamia (but including also Egypt). Eventually, Ashurbanipal reconquered Babylon, and the coalition disbanded. Ashurbanipal also crushed a rebell
1 CONC ion in Egypt, and conquered Elam, destroying its capital city, Susa. He also conquered a great part of the Arab territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT After defeating the Babylonian coalition, in his 22nd year, he appointed Kandalanu as puppet-king of Babylon. However, some evidence would suggest that Ashurbanipal and Kandalanu are the same person, and that he simply decided to 
1 CONC use a fictitious name for the kingship over Babylon.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the final decade of his rule, Assyria was quite peaceful, but the country apparently faced a serious decline. Documentation from the last years of reign of Ashurbanipal is very scarce, and even the date of his death is not 
1 CONC known with certainty. The latest attestations for Ashurbanipal come only to his year 38 (631 BC), but later sources have him reigning for 42 years (to 627 BC). It has been suggested that the last years of Assurbanipal witnessed a 
1 CONC struggle between the aged king and two of his rebellious sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Ashurbanipal opened the way to catastrophic strife between his sons for the throne of Assyria. The contenders included Ashur-etil-ilani, Sin-shar-ishkun and Sin-shumu-lishir, who were probably his sons, and the eventu
1 CONC al new king of Babylon, Nabopolassar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Oppenheim's dates (as given here) are drawn from references in the inscription from Harran of the mother of Nabonidus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashurbanipal is one of the most popular Assyrian kings, as his name is often used for boys within the Assyrian communities today.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT See also
1 CONT Death of Sardanapalus, a 1827 painting by Eugène Delacroix. 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Esarhaddon King of Assyria
1 CONT 669–627 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-etil-ilani 
0 @N6000000001503906268@ NOTE The Assyrians ruled Upper Asia[110] for five hundred and twenty years,
1 CONT
1 CONT and from them the Medes were the first who made revolt. These having
1 CONT
1 CONT fought for their freedom with the Assyrians proved themselves good
1 CONT
1 CONT men, and thus they pushed off the yoke of slavery from themselves and
1 CONT
1 CONT were set free; and after them the other nations also did the same as
1 CONT
1 CONT the Medes: and when all on the continent were thus independent, they
1 CONT
1 CONT returned again to despotic rule as follows:--96. There appeared among
1 CONT
1 CONT the Medes a man of great ability whose name was Deïokes, and this man
1 CONT
1 CONT was the son of Phraortes. This Deïokes, having formed a desire for
1 CONT
1 CONT despotic power, did thus:--whereas the Medes dwelt in separate
1 CONT
1 CONT villages, he, being even before that time of great repute in his own
1 CONT
1 CONT village, set himself to practise just dealing much more and with
1 CONT
1 CONT greater zeal than before; and this he did although there was much
1 CONT
1 CONT lawlessness throughout the whole of Media, and although he knew that
1 CONT
1 CONT injustice is ever at feud with justice. And the Medes of the same
1 CONT
1 CONT village, seeing his manners, chose him for their judge. So he, since
1 CONT
1 CONT he was aiming at power, was upright and just, and doing thus he had no
1 CONT
1 CONT little praise from his fellow-citizens, insomuch that those of the
1 CONT
1 CONT other villages learning that Deïokes was a man who more than all
1 CONT
1 CONT others gave decision rightly, whereas before this they had been wont
1 CONT
1 CONT to suffer from unjust judgments, themselves also when they heard it
1 CONT
1 CONT came gladly to Deïokes to have their causes determined, and at last
1 CONT
1 CONT they trusted the business to no one else. 97. Then, as more and more
1 CONT
1 CONT continually kept coming to him, because men learnt that his decisions
1 CONT
1 CONT proved to be according to the truth, Deïokes perceiving that
1 CONT
1 CONT everything was referred to himself would no longer sit in the place
1 CONT
1 CONT where he used formerly to sit in public to determine causes, and said
1 CONT
1 CONT that he would determine causes no more, for it was not profitable for
1 CONT
1 CONT him to neglect his own affairs and to determine causes for his
1 CONT
1 CONT neighbours all through the day. So then, since robbery and lawlessness
1 CONT
1 CONT prevailed even much more in the villages than they did before, the
1 CONT
1 CONT Medes having assembled together in one place considered with one
1 CONT
1 CONT another and spoke about the state in which they were: and I suppose
1 CONT
1 CONT the friends of Deïokes spoke much to this effect: "Seeing that we are
1 CONT
1 CONT not able to dwell in the land under the present order of things, let
1 CONT
1 CONT us set up a king from among ourselves, and thus the land will be well
1 CONT
1 CONT governed and we ourselves shall turn to labour, and shall not be
1 CONT
1 CONT ruined by lawlessness." By some such words as these they persuaded
1 CONT
1 CONT themselves to have a king. 98. And when they straightway proposed the
1 CONT
1 CONT question whom they should set up to be king, Deïokes was much put
1 CONT
1 CONT forward and commended by every one, until at last they agreed that he
1 CONT
1 CONT should be their king. And he bade them build for him a palace worthy
1 CONT
1 CONT of the royal dignity and strengthen him with a guard of spearmen. And
1 CONT
1 CONT the Medes did so: for they built him a large and strong palace in that
1 CONT
1 CONT part of the land which he told them, and they allowed him to select
1 CONT
1 CONT spearmen from all the Medes. And when he had obtained the rule over
1 CONT
1 CONT them, he compelled the Medes to make one fortified city and pay chief
1 CONT
1 CONT attention to this, having less regard to the other cities. And as the
1 CONT
1 CONT Medes obeyed him in this also, he built large and strong walls, those
1 CONT
1 CONT which are now called Agbatana, standing in circles one within the
1 CONT
1 CONT other. And this wall is so contrived that one circle is higher than
1 CONT
1 CONT the next by the height of the battlements alone. And to some extent, I
1 CONT
1 CONT suppose, the nature of the ground, seeing that it is on a hill,
1 CONT
1 CONT assists towards this end; but much more was it produced by art, since
1 CONT
1 CONT the circles are in all seven in number.[111] And within the last
1 CONT
1 CONT circle are the royal palace and the treasure-houses. The largest of
1 CONT
1 CONT these walls is in size about equal to the circuit of the wall round
1 CONT
1 CONT Athens; and of the first circle the battlements are white, of the
1 CONT
1 CONT second black, of the third crimson, of the fourth blue, of the fifth
1 CONT
1 CONT red: thus are the battlements of all the circles coloured with various
1 CONT
1 CONT tints, and the two last have their battlements one of them overlaid
1 CONT
1 CONT with silver and the other with gold. 99. These walls then Deïokes
1 CONT
1 CONT built for himself and round his own palace, and the people he
1 CONT
1 CONT commanded to dwell round about the wall. And after all was built,
1 CONT
1 CONT Deïokes established the rule, which he was the first to establish,
1 CONT
1 CONT ordaining that none should enter into the presence of the king, but
1 CONT
1 CONT that they deal with him always through messengers; and that the king
1 CONT
1 CONT should be seen by no one; and moreover that to laugh or to spit in
1 CONT
1 CONT presence is unseemly, and this last for every one without
1 CONT
1 CONT exception.[112] Now he surrounded himself with this state[113] to the
1 CONT
1 CONT end that his fellows, who had been brought up with him and were of no
1 CONT
1 CONT meaner family nor behind him in manly virtue, might not be grieved by
1 CONT
1 CONT seeing him and make plots against him, but that being unseen by them
1 CONT
1 CONT he might be thought to be of different mould. 100. Having set these
1 CONT
1 CONT things in order and strengthened himself in his despotism, he was
1 CONT
1 CONT severe in preserving justice; and the people used to write down their
1 CONT
1 CONT causes and send them in to his presence, and he determined the
1 CONT
1 CONT questions which were brought in to him and sent them out again. Thus
1 CONT
1 CONT he used to do about the judgment of causes; and he also took order for
1 CONT
1 CONT this, that is to say, if he heard that any one was behaving in an
1 CONT
1 CONT unruly manner, he sent for him and punished him according as each act
1 CONT
1 CONT of wrong deserved, and he had watchers and listeners about all the
1 CONT
1 CONT land over which he ruled.
1 CONT
1 CONT 101. Deïokes then united the Median race alone, and was ruler of this:
1 CONT
1 CONT and of the Medes there are the tribes which here follow, namely,
1 CONT
1 CONT Busai, Paretakenians, Struchates, Arizantians, Budians, Magians: the
1 CONT
1 CONT tribes of the Medes are so many in number. 102. Now the son of Deïokes
1 CONT
1 CONT was Phraortes, who when Deïokes was dead, having been king for three-
1 CONT
1 CONT and-fifty years,
0 @N6000000002187613920@ NOTE Deioces
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Deioces, Déjocès or Diyako (709 to 656 B.C.) was the first Aryan king of the Medes, an Iranian people in what would become Iran. He united seven Median tribes and became their judge and leader, beginning in 701 BC. After seven yea
1 CONC rs of rule he resigned, the Medes elected him as king until 665 BC. Deioces built a palace in the capital, Ecbatana, now known as Hamadan. The story of his rise to power is told in book one of The History by Herodotus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT — King of Persia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Phraortes 
