0 @N6000000002187705511@ NOTE Mutakkil-Nusku
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mutakkil-Nusku was King of Assyria briefly in 1133 BC. The son of Ashur-dan I, Mutakkil-Nusku usurped the throne from his brother, Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur, apparently only shortly after their father's death. Mutakkil-Nusku died soon
1 CONC  after this act of usurpation, leaving the throne to his son, Ashur-resh-ishi I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1133 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-resh-ishi I 
0 @N6000000002187705529@ NOTE Ashur-dan I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-dan I was one of the longest-reigning Kings of Assyria, reigning for some 46 years according to the Assyrian King List (although one version of the list gives him only 36 years). According to one of the more commonly used ch
1 CONC ronologies of the middle Assyrian period, he reigned from 1179 BC to 1133 BC. He succeeded his father, Ninurta-apal-Ekur, and was succeeded by his son, Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur. Beyond this, little is known of his long reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ninurta-apal-Ekur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1179–1133 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur 
0 @N6000000002187705548@ NOTE Ninurta-apal-Ekur
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ninurta-apal-Ekur was a King of Assyria. There is some dispute as to how long he reigned, based on disputes among various copies of the Assyrian King List. The Nassouhi King List, sometimes considered to be older than the other ve
1 CONC rsions of the King List we have, gives him 13 years of reign, but the other king lists give him only 3. More recent scholarship has tended to support the shorter reign, in which case he reigned from 1182 to 1179 BC (alternately, h
1 CONC e reigned from 1193 to 1180 BC). He was a usurper, the great-great-great grandson of Adad-nirari I. He seized the throne from Enlil-kudurri-usur, a son of the powerful king Tukulti-Ninurta I. After his reign of either three or thi
1 CONC rteen years, Ninurta-apal-Ekur was succeeded by his son, Ashur-dan I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Enlil-kudurri-usur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1182–1179 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-dan I 
0 @N6000000002187705599@ NOTE Shalmaneser I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shalmaneser I, son of Adad-nirari I, succeeded his father as king of Assyria about 1310 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT He carried on a series of campaigns against the Aramaeans in northern Mesopotamia, annexed a portion of Cilicia to the Assyrian empire, and established Assyrian colonies on the borders of Cappadocia.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to his annals, discovered at Assur, in his first year he conquered eight countries in the north-west and destroyed the fortress of Arinnu, the dust of which he brought to Assur. In his second year he defeated Shattuara I
1 CONC I, king of Hanilgalbat, and his Hittite and Ahlamu allies, and conquered the whole country from Taidu to Irridu, all of mount Kashiar to Eluhat and the fortresses of Sudu, Harranu to Carchemish on the Euphrates. He built palaces a
1 CONC t Assur and Nineveh, restored "the world-temple" at Assur, and founded the city of Kalah.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Adad-nirari I King of Assyria
1 CONT 1274–1245 BCE Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tukulti-Ninurta I 
0 @N6000000002187705618@ NOTE Adad-nirari I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Three kings of Assyria were named Adad-Nirari. This stela represents one of them.Adad-nirari I (1305 BC (?) - 1274 BC (?)) was a king of Assyria. He is the earliest Assyrian king whose annals survive in any detail.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adad-nirari I was a king of substantial military consequence in the development of the Assyrian kingdom. He conquered northern Mesopotamia and subdued the Mitanni. His surviving records indicate that he defeated King Nazi-marutash
1 CONC  at the Battle of Kar-Ishtar, and also defeated kings named Shattuara I and his son Wasashatta.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External link
1 CONT Outline of Assyrian history by E. J. de Meester 
0 @N6000000000437345135@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Georgia
0 @N6000000000437682844@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Georgia
0 @N6000000002187705653@ NOTE Ashur-uballit I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-uballit I, or Assur-uballit I, was king of the Assyrian empire (c. 1365 BC to 1330 BC). His reign marks Assyria's independence from the kingdom of Mitanni, by defeating Shuttarna II; and the beginning of Assyria's emergence 
1 CONC as a powerful empire. Later on, due to disorder in Babylonia following the death of Burnaburiash II, Ashur-uballit established his grandson Kurigalzu II on the Babylonian throne, in the first of what would become a series of Assyr
1 CONC ian interventions in Babylonian affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the Amarna letters, a series of diplomatic letters from various Middle Eastern monarchs to Amenhotep III and Akhenaten of Egypt, we find two letters from Ashur-uballit I, the second being a follow-up letter to the first. In t
1 CONC he letters, Ashur-uballit refers to his second predecessor Ashur-nadin-ahhe II as his "father" or "ancestor," rather than his actual father, Eriba-Adad I, which has led some critics of conventional Egyptian chronology, such as Dav
1 CONC id Rohl, to claim that the Ashur-uballit of the Amarna letters was not the same as Ashur-uballit I. This, however, ignores the fact that monarchs in the Amarna letters frequently refer to predecessors as their "father," even if th
1 CONC ey were not their biological sons. In this case, Ashur-uballit presumably referred to Ashur-nadin-ahhe because the latter, unlike Eriba-Adad I, had previously corresponded with the Egyptian court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eriba-Adad I King of Assyria
1 CONT 1365–1330 BCE Succeeded by:
1 CONT Enlil-nirari 
